### Issue description

[ColorPalette](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_colorpalette.html) inherits `Resource.changed`, but modifying its `colors` property does not emit the `changed` signal.
This makes it difficult for objects which depend on a `ColorPalette` resource to react when the palette is edited.

#### Example

An editor plugin may expose a `ColorPalette` subresource and show a preview of its colors.
The containing resource/control can connect to `palette.changed`, but editing `ColorPalette.colors` does not notify it.

The current 4.7 implementation of [ColorPalette::set_colors()](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/4.7/scene/resources/color_palette.cpp#L35-L37) only assigns the array:

```cpp
void ColorPalette::set_colors(const PackedColorArray &p_colors) {
    colors = p_colors;
}
```

For comparison, [Gradient::set_colors()](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/4.7/scene/resources/gradient.cpp#L156-L165) calls `emit_changed()` after updating its colors:

```cpp
void Gradient::set_colors(const Vector<Color> &p_colors) {
	if (points.size() < p_colors.size()) {
		is_sorted = false;
	}

	points.resize(p_colors.size());
	for (uint32_t i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) {
		points[i].color = p_colors[i];
	}

	emit_changed();
}
```

Note that [ColorPalette.colors](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_colorpalette.html#class-colorpalette-property-colors) returning a copied PackedColorArray does not explain or justify the missing Resource.changed signal.
Also, although the Gradient example uses `Vector<Color>`, Godot defines `PackedColorArray` as an alias--so the comparison holds.

